  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening Livelihood and Food Security of Rohingya Refugees and Hosts</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The goal of this project is to increase production of diversified fresh and nutritious food that will be impacted positively on camp residents&apos; daily lives and improved physical and mental health as well as reduce malnutrition. This project will be implemented in Partnership with a long -standing local Partner, Mukti Cox&apos;s Bazar who has significant experience and expertise in implementing climate -adaptive homestead gardening projects among Cox&apos;s Bazar Rohingya and host communities. 75% of the project participants (1125 Households) will be selected from Rohingya camps and 25% (375 Households) from hosts.  With recent World Food Programme funding cuts having come into effect June 2023, many Rohingya households as well as Bangladeshi host community households adjacent to the camps are struggling to put food on the table. Families are frequently resorting to negative coping strategies, including skipping meals, eating less nutritious and balanced foods, cutting down on other critical household items like soap and oil, selling household assets to buy food, or resorting to high interest loans to buy food. About two -thirds of households in the camps (65%) and half of the households in the Bangladeshi host community (55%) relied on less preferred or less expensive food as the most frequently used coping strategy for both populations (REVA 6 June 2023}. In the past 27% of recipients of World Food Programme food rations sold a portion of these rations to buy fresh food and other needed items. This has decreased to less than 6% as food rations have been reduced significantly and food has become scarce.  Among households that sold food packages, 27% sold food to purchase other food items, mostly less preferred or less expensive food. Food remains the top -most priority for both Rohingya and Bangladeshi host communities; 80% of Rohingya households and 57% of Bangladeshi community households cited the need for greater access to fresh food and the continuation of food assistance. Th</narrative>
  </description>
